# See https://help.github.com/articles/ignoring-files/ for more about ignoring files.

# dependencies
/node_modules
/.pnp
.pnp.*
.yarn/*
!.yarn/patches
!.yarn/plugins
!.yarn/releases
!.yarn/versions

# testing
/coverage

# next.js
/.next/
/out/

# production
/build

# misc
.DS_Store
*.pem

# debug
npm-debug.log*
yarn-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*
.pnpm-debug.log*

# env files (can opt-in for committing if needed)
.env*
!.env.example

# vercel
.vercel

# typescript
*.tsbuildinfo
next-env.d.ts

# local working files — lead CSVs, import sheets, one-off exports. Untracked
# but not ignored is the dangerous combination: the directory holds real
# names, emails and phone numbers, and it was one `git add -A` away from
# being public on a repository that anyone can read.
/scratchpad

# Screenshots from scripts/screenshots — look at them, don't commit them.
.screenshots/

# The same hazard, except it had already happened. /output held 45 committed
# files — 18 CSVs and 11 TSVs of prospect data, ~33,000 rows carrying 1,025
# distinct email addresses, direct phone numbers, contact names and roles,
# hand-written sales notes and per-lead estimated deal values — on a public
# repository. The scrape scripts beside them (.mjs) name the sources and the
# method, and output/pdf held a generated document.
#
# Nothing in app/, lib/ or components/ reads this directory: it is where the
# lead-harvesting scripts write, and that output belongs on a laptop, not in
# version control. Ignored as a directory rather than by extension, so the
# next export lands here and stays put whatever it is called.
#
# NOTE: untracking these removed them from the current tree, not from the
# repository's history. Every commit that added them still contains them and
# is still fetchable by anyone. See SECURITY-NOTES.md — the history rewrite
# and the disclosure decision are the owner's calls to make, not something a
# .gitignore line closes out.
/output
